flowchart TD
  %% author: Brad W. Setser | outlet: CFR Follow the Money | date: 2025-11-12
  %% title: China's Massive Surplus is Everywhere (Yet The IMF Still Has Trouble Seeing It Clearly)
  %% url: https://www.cfr.org/articles/chinas-massive-surplus-everywhere-yet-imf-still-has-trouble-seeing-it-clearly
  %% topics: China, IMF, global imbalances, fiscal policy | genre: blog / institutional critique

  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["`**Question**
      How big is China's surplus really, why does the IMF miss it, and what policy should follow?`"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["`**Stated assumption**
      The surplus stems from a shortfall in China's domestic demand (European Central Bank view)`"]
    P2["`**Stated assumption**
      Deflation combined with a rising trade surplus calls for sustained fiscal expansion, not monetary easing`"]
    P3["`**Hidden assumption**
      *External balance is a legitimate policy target; exporting your way out of a slump imposes costs on other countries*`"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    E1["`**Data**
      Export volumes up 40 percent since end-2019 versus imports up 1 percent; manufactured goods surplus over $2 trillion (about 10.5 percent of GDP); goods surplus $1.2 trillion; car exports over 6 million, heading toward 8 million in 2026; current account surplus 2.2 percent of GDP versus the EU's 2.6 percent; IMF forecast a 2025 surplus of $375 billion, while the actual tops $650 billion through the third quarter`"]
    E2["`**Studies and sources**
      IMF External Sector Report 2025, October World Economic Outlook and 2024 Article IV report; European Central Bank analysis; Netherlands Bureau for Economic Policy Analysis export volume data; Microsoft annual report`"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["`**Demand shortfall**
      Weak domestic demand leaves spare capacity for exports; rate cuts lower yields and weaken the yuan, widening the surplus`"]
    M2["`**Mismeasurement**
      The 2022 balance of payments methodology and the unexplained income deficit each cut about 1 percentage point of GDP from the reported current account surplus`"]
    M3["`**IMF lens**
      Relying only on the reported current account frames China's problem as purely internal, so the IMF prescribes monetary easing`"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["`**Intermediate claim**
      China's surplus is massive and growing, yet IMF policy advice effectively lets China export its way out of its housing slump`"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["`**Main claim**
      The IMF should recognize China's external imbalance and call for a fiscal expansion funded by the central government`"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Counterpoints"]
    X["`**Opposing view**
      The IMF's framing; the argument that Europe's surplus is almost as big`"]
    QF["`**Caveat**
      China's GDP data are too thin to confirm the net export math; the IMF does support pro-consumption structural reforms`"]
    R1["`**Other explanation**
      Ireland distorts European data; IMF forecast misses come from ignoring lagged exchange rate effects and treating the pandemic surge as temporary`"]
  end

  P1 --> M1
  E1 --> M1
  E1 --> M2
  E2 --> M2
  E2 --> M3
  M2 --> M3
  M1 --> C1
  M3 --> C1
  P2 --> K
  P3 -->|rule behind it| C1
  C1 --> K
  K -.->|pushes back on| X
  K -. caveat .- QF
  M3 -. other explanation .- R1

  Q ~~~ P1 & P2 & P3
  P1 ~~~ E1
  P3 ~~~ E2

